{"text": " You wake up in 1941 in Oxford. It's wartime and the lights are low. Did you know that the scientists here collect a patient's urine to get the medicine back and use it again? They're running out. Here's your patient. Albert Alexander, a police officer with an infection, nothing can stop. On the 12th of February, he gets a new drug, penicillin. Within a day, he's better. But there isn't enough. After five days, it's gone. He gets worse again and dies in March. By one account, four of the next five patients recover. The idea works. The supply is what fails. How long do you live? In Britain, a newborn could expect about 61 years. So what is medicine now? A cure, a drug that finds the germ and leaves you alone. It helps a handful of people. But how do you prove a drug works for everyone? Seven years later, someone finds a way.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.72, "text": " You wake up in 1941 in Oxford.", "tokens": [50364, 509, 6634, 493, 294, 35364, 294, 24786, 13, 50538], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11848208921175477, "compression_ratio": 1.518032786885246, "no_speech_prob": 0.012757527641952038, "words": [{"word": " You", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.12, "probability": 0.7205362915992737}, {"word": " wake", "start": 0.12, "end": 0.32, "probability": 0.9884195327758789}, {"word": " up", "start": 0.32, "end": 0.56, "probability": 0.9995256662368774}, {"word": " in", "start": 0.56, "end": 0.7, "probability": 0.9964630007743835}, {"word": " 1941", "start": 0.7, "end": 1.48, "probability": 0.9848649501800537}, {"word": " in", "start": 1.48, "end": 2.34, "probability": 0.8709281086921692}, {"word": " Oxford.", "start": 2.34, "end": 2.72, "probability": 0.9917940497398376}]}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 3.2400000000000064, "end": 5.08, "text": " It's wartime and the lights are low.", "tokens": [50538, 467, 311, 45124, 1312, 293, 264, 5811, 366, 2295, 13, 50652], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.11848208921175477, "compression_